    "message": {
     "content": "### 1. Assessment of the Offer\n\nAt a high level, this is a **solid baseline offer, but not a top-of-band offer for Google L6 (Senior PM) in the Bay Area.** Because you are already an IC6 at Meta making ~$526k/yr and are happy in your current role, this offer is **too close to a lateral comp move over 4 years to accept as-is.**\n\n---\n\n### 2. Breakdown of the Numbers\n\n#### A. The Real Value & Google\u2019s Vesting Schedule\nGoogle does **not** vest initial new-hire equity evenly (25%/year). Google uses a **front-loaded schedule** (typically 33% in Year 1, 33% in Year 2, 22% in Year 3, and 12% in Year 4):\n\n| Component | Year 1 | Year 2 | Year 3 | Year 4 |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Base Salary** | $235,000 | $235,000 | $235,000 | $235,000 |\n| **Bonus (20% target)** | $47,000 | $47,000 | $47,000 | $47,000 |\n| **RSUs ($1.08M grant)** | $356,400 *(33%)* | $356,400 *(33%)* | $237,600 *(22%)* | $129,600 *(12%)* |\n| **Total Comp (Pre-Refresh)** | **$638,400** | **$638,400** | **$519,600** | **$411,600** |\n\n* **4-Year Annualized Average:** ~$552,000/yr (only ~5% above your current $526,000/yr Meta compensation).\n* **Years 1 & 2:** You get an immediate cash-flow boost (~$112,000/yr more than Meta).\n* **Years 3 & 4 (The Cliff):** Your initial grant drops significantly. While annual refreshers (which vest 25%/yr) help fill the gap, they depend entirely on performance ratings and rarely create a massive surplus over your current Meta trajectory unless you are consistently rated at the top of the curve.\n\n#### B. Component-by-Component Reality Check\n* **Base Salary ($235k):** Below your current Meta base ($240k) and sitting near the midpoint of Google's posted range ($183k\u2013$271k). There is substantial headroom here.\n* **Sign-on Bonus ($0):** Leaving a company where you have $200k in unvested RSUs over the next 12 months with $0 in sign-on bonus is a concession you should not make.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Recommendation: Negotiate Firmly\n\n**Do not accept, and do not walk away yet\u2014negotiate.**\n\nYou hold maximum leverage:\n1. **Passive candidate:** They reached out to you; you have no urgency to leave.\n2. **Strong alternative:** You are an established IC6 at Meta with steady momentum and an even 25% annual vesting schedule.\n3. **Band headroom:** The offer has clear room to move across base, equity, and sign-on.\n\n---\n\n### 4. What Exactly to Ask For\n\n| Component | Current Offer | Target Counter | Rationale |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Base Salary** | $235,000 | **$255,000 \u2013 $260,000** | Below your current $240k; upper band reaches $271k. |\n| **Sign-On Bonus** | $0 | **$75,000 \u2013 $100,000** | Offsets lost near-term Meta vesting ($200k in next 12 mos) and transition risk. |\n| **RSU Grant** | $1,080,000 | **$1,250,000 \u2013 $1,300,000** | Pushes Y1/Y2 TC to ~$700k+ and cushions the Y3/Y4 equity drop. |\n\n---\n\n### 5. How to Frame the Negotiation (Script / Talking Points)\n\nWhen you speak to the recruiter, anchor on your enthusiasm for the role while being transparent about the financial hurdle:\n\n> *\"I\u2019m genuinely excited about the team and the scope of this role. However, as I mentioned early on, I\u2019m in a great position at Meta making around $526k total comp with $240k base and $200k of RSUs vesting over the next 12 months on a very predictable schedule.*\n>\n> *Looking at the initial offer, the base salary is slightly below what I currently earn ($235k vs $240k), and without a sign-on bonus, making the transition creates near-term risk. Over a 4-year average, the numbers are essentially lateral to what I already have.*\n>\n> *If we can bridge this gap, I would love to sign. To make this move compelling:*\n> 1. *Adjust the **base salary to $255,000** (reflecting the higher end of the level's band).*\n> 2. *Include a **sign-on bonus of $80,000** to offset the equity I'm walking away from in the next 12 months.*\n> 3. *Increase the **RSU grant to $1,250,000** over 4 years to account for long-term parity.*\n>\n> *If we can get to that package, I'm ready to accept and give notice.\"*\n\n### Bottom Line\nIf Google moves on at least two of these three levers (e.g., adds a $75k+ sign-on bonus and increases the RSU grant to ~$1.2M+), it becomes a compelling financial upgrade. If they refuse to move on any component, staying at Meta is the lower-risk, higher-certainty choice.",
